{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "To date, fewer studies in published literature have focussed on examining in depth the socio-demographic, environmental, lifestyle and dietary patterns and other factors contributing to nutrition transition in Pakistan mainly in the women of reproductive years.",
      "reason": "The Introduction describes the current state of knowledge (nutrition transition, rising obesity, survey findings) and explicitly notes the limited number of studies and gaps in understanding determinants among Pakistani women."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_1",
      "quote": "Little research has been done in Pakistan to explore nutrition transition, and its associated nutrition challenges. The current study attempted to investigate the socio-economic, environmental and demographic determinants of rise in obesity among women of reproductive years (15-49 years) in Pakistan.",
      "reason": "The Abstract and Introduction state the paucity of research in Pakistan and explicitly justify the need for this review to investigate determinants and inform policy."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No text in the provided evidence explains whether similar systematic reviews exist, nor cites or contrasts prior reviews to justify this review as an update or replication."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "To date, fewer studies in published literature have focussed on examining in depth the socio-demographic, environmental, lifestyle and dietary patterns and other factors contributing to nutrition transition in Pakistan mainly in the women of reproductive years. This review article was planned to highlight the nutrition transition in Pakistan by exploring the factors contributing to rise in overweight and obesity among women of reproductive age (15-49 years), and possible policy recommendation for policy-makers to deal with this challenge.",
      "reason": "The review addresses determinants of obesity and nutrition transition rather than evaluating intervention effects, so describing an intervention mechanism is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "This review article was planned to highlight the nutrition transition in Pakistan by exploring the factors contributing to rise in overweight and obesity among women of reproductive age (15-49 years), and possible policy recommendation for policy-makers to deal with this challenge.",
      "reason": "The review does not evaluate complex interventions or delivery contexts; it synthesises determinants, so inclusion of a logic model for intervention components is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_1",
      "quote": "The current study attempted to investigate the socio-economic, environmental and demographic determinants of rise in obesity among women of reproductive years (15-49 years) in Pakistan.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the review objective (investigate socio-economic, environmental and demographic determinants of rising obesity among women 15–49 in Pakistan), but does not present the objective framed using a specific question-formulation framework (e.g., PICO, PICOS, PECOS) as requested by this element."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "We systemically reviewed the published literature to identify studies regarding nutrition transition, including overweight and obesity, for a period of 10 years from 2005 to 2015.",
      "reason": "This review addresses determinants of obesity (observational/explanatory focus), not effects of interventions; therefore planned comparisons using PICO for intervention effects are not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "We systemically reviewed the published literature to identify studies regarding nutrition transition, including overweight and obesity, for a period of 10 years from 2005 to 2015.PubMed and PubGet were used as resource database. Search key terms were \"Socioeconomic status\", \"nutrition\", \"middle and low income countries\", language, having an abstract and describing factors contributing towards overweight and obesity. ... Out of these, 20(74%) studies were excluded as 8(29%) did not report results stratified by SES; 2(7.5%) restricted to adolescents; 10(37%) did not include body mass index (BMI) as an outcome. One (3.7%) study was further excluded restricted to obesity in males. Finally, 6(22.2%) studies met the eligibility criteria and were entered in data extraction form (Table-1).",
      "reason": "Methods state timeframe (2005–2015), databases searched, search terms, language/abstract requirement, population exclusions (adolescents, males) and outcome requirement (BMI). However, eligible study designs, explicit settings, and follow-up requirements are not clearly specified as eligibility criteria, so not all study-characteristic elements are reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "We systemically reviewed the published literature to identify studies regarding nutrition transition, including overweight and obesity, for a period of 10 years from 2005 to 2015.PubMed and PubGet were used as resource database. Search key terms were \"Socioeconomic status\", \"nutrition\", \"middle and low income countries\", language, having an abstract and describing factors contributing towards overweight and obesity. ... We included all papers published in the English",
      "reason": "The review specifies a publication year range (2005–2015) and indicates inclusion of English-language papers, but does not report other report-characteristic restrictions (e.g. publication status, grey literature) or fully complete the sentence on language; thus the criterion is only partially met."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Out of these, 20(74%) studies were excluded as 8(29%) did not report results stratified by SES; 2(7.5%) restricted to adolescents; 10(37%) did not include body mass index (BMI) as an outcome.",
      "reason": "The methods explicitly separate exclusions for studies that \"did not include body mass index (BMI) as an outcome\" from those that \"did not report results stratified by SES\", distinguishing outcome not measured from outcome not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "We used the World Health Organisation (WHO) recommended BMI cut-offs to categorise BMI into following groups: <18.5 as underweight, 18.5-24.9 as normal, 25-29.9 as overweight and >29.9 as obese. ... Figure-1: Flow chart for the syntheses of systematic review.",
      "reason": "The review defines BMI categories used in analysis and presents a flow chart of study selection; it also presents analyses by socioeconomic groups in results. However, there is no explicit statement in Methods that specifies the exact groups used for each synthesis and links them back to the comparisons articulated in the review objectives, so reporting is incomplete."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No rationale is provided in the Methods for notable restrictions such as limiting to 2005–2015 or English-language papers; the text does not explain why those eligibility restrictions were applied."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 80
}

